# Coding Standards

The rules worth knowing before you write anything. The **full suite**, with a stable ID and an
honest enforcement marker per rule, is [`standards/README.md`](standards/README.md) — cite
`BE-004` in a review comment rather than paraphrasing it.

Enforced by CI (`make check`) and code review; the summary lives in [`../CLAUDE.md`](../CLAUDE.md) §3.

> A green pipeline means every `[CI]` rule held. It says nothing about the `[Review]` ones, which
> are the majority. That distinction is the whole point of the markers.

## Structure

- **File length:** target ≤ 300 lines, hard cap **400**. Over → split by concern. (BE-001)
- **Function length:** ≤ 50 lines. Cyclomatic complexity ≤ 10, enforced by ruff `C90`. (BE-002, BE-003)
- **One router per resource.** Route handlers are thin; business logic lives in `services/`, data
  access in the DB layer. No fat controllers. (BE-005, API-001)
- **No dead code, no `.bak` files, no commented-out blocks** committed. (BE-006)

## Python

- **Type everything.** `mypy --strict` passes. No untyped public functions. (BE-004)
- **Formatting:** `black` (line length 100). **Linting:** `ruff` (E, F, I, B, UP, N, S, C90). (BE-010, BE-011)
- **Security:** `bandit` clean; `pip-audit` clean. Never `eval`/`exec`, `shell=True`, or unsafe
  deserialization — Semgrep `dangerous-ops` blocks these. (SEC-010, SEC-011, SEC-014)
- **Async-first** for I/O. Prefer the ORM over raw SQL; scope every `DELETE`/`UPDATE` with `WHERE`.
  (BE-012, BE-013, BE-014)
